  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "The coefficient of ${a}^{3}$ in the expansion of ${(1-\\frac{a}{2})}^{14}$ is",
  "worked_solution": "The binomial expansion for $(1+x)^{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2!}{x}^{2}+\u22ef.\n$$\n\nIn particular, the ${x}^{3}$ term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{n(n-1)(n-2)}{3!}{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-\\frac{a}{2}$ and $n$ with $14$ in the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{14(14-1)(14-2)}{3!}\\cdot {(-\\frac{a}{2})}^{3} & =\\frac{14\\cdot 13\\cdot 12}{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}})\\cdot {a}^{3} \\\\  & =364\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8}){a}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{91}{2}{a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${a}^{3}$ is $-\\frac{91}{2}.$",
